  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I reviewed all 7 patients on the DOAC dose review list (week of 10/19/2026) under RBC-PHARM-07. Three DOAC doses changed, two dose changes were reversed by results that came in during the review, and two patients' doses were already correct. Each dose change has a progress note citing the records it relies on.\n\n1. Irene Castellano (pt-0061), apixaban 2.5 → 5 mg twice daily. Her itraconazole course ended 08/14 (ID letter 9/10). She is 74, weighs 78 kg, SCr 1.02, so she meets none of the dose criteria.\n2. Frank Delgado (pt-0003), rivaroxaban stays at 20 mg daily (reduction reversed). I first lowered it to 15 mg based on a St. Brigid creatinine of 160 µmol/L (CrCl 44). St. Brigid then corrected that result to 118 µmol/L (1.33 mg/dL) after an analyzer calibration fault. That gives CrCl 59.9, so I put him back on 20 mg and wrote a correction note.\n3. Raymond Duquette (pt-0041), apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. He meets no dose criteria (70, 84 kg, SCr 0.98), but has an active itraconazole order from 10/06.\n4. Norman Fairweather (pt-0062), apixaban 5 mg twice daily is correct; no change. He meets one criterion (age 82). Two St. Brigid creatinines came in during the review: 150 µmol/L on 10/21, then 100 µmol/L (1.13 mg/dL) on 10/22. The newer value is the one that counts. I wrote a review note.\n5. Eugene Moravec (pt-0038), rivaroxaban 15 → 20 mg daily. Using actual weight as the policy requires (96 kg, age 79, SCr 1.45), CrCl is 56.1. The May reduction was based on ideal body weight. He then sent a portal message with a home scale weight of 185 lb. A self-reported weight doesn't count under the policy, so the decision stands (addendum note).\n6. Marjorie Pellegrino (pt-0043), rivaroxaban 20 → 15 mg daily. A new creatinine of 1.25 (10/22) came in during the review, giving CrCl 43.6 (female, 75, 71 kg).\n7. Harold Vance (pt-0002), apixaban stays at 2.5 mg twice daily, now because of itraconazole. He meets only the age criterion (82; 64.9 kg, SCr 1.21), so I first raised him to 5 mg. A St. Brigid dermatology letter then arrived: itraconazole 200 mg daily for 12 weeks, started 10/22. Under the policy that means 2.5 mg twice daily, so I lowered it back and wrote a correction note.\n\nNo other medications were changed, including the itraconazole. I closed the 5 inbox items that arrived during the review, each linked to the order or note that resolves it.\n\nThings for you:\n- **Re-review when itraconazole ends:** Duquette's course started 10/06 and Vance's 10/22 (about mid-January 2027). I didn't create reminders for either.\n- **Other open inbox items:** two critical potassiums are still waiting in your inbox: pt-0016 (6.3, 10/20) and pt-0008 (6.4, 10/22). The other items there from before the review are also still open.",
